Processes and signals: starting programs, waiting for them, identities, resource limits and
usage, and signal handling.

### Starting and waiting

| Function | Does |
|---|---|
| `sys::posix_spawn(path, argv, envp, actions, options)` | starts a program as a child, returns its pid |
| `sys::posix_spawnp(file, argv, envp, actions, options)` | the same, looking file up in PATH |
| `sys::fork()` | duplicates the process: the child's pid in the parent, 0 in the child |
| `sys::execve`, `sys::execvp` | replace the process image |
| `sys::waitpid(pid)` | waits for a child and reaps it: a `wait_status` |
| `sys::try_waitpid(pid)` | the same without blocking: nothing while the child runs |
| `sys::exit(status)`, `sys::exit_immediately(status)` | end the process, with or without atexit handlers |

`argv` and `envp` are null-terminated arrays of mutable C strings, as POSIX takes them; a null
`envp` passes the current environment. A `wait_status` tells which child ended (`child()`, what
a wait for -1 or `-group` needs) and how: `exit_code()` and `signal()` are optionals,
`succeeded()` means exit code 0.

`spawn_actions` lists descriptor changes the child applies before running the program (dup2,
close, open), so a child's standard streams can be redirected without fork. It follows ca's
explicit lifecycle: `make()` builds it, `cleanup()` frees it; it moves but does not copy.
`spawn_options` is a plain value (the default changes nothing): `set_process_group(group)` has
the child join group first, or with 0 lead a new one, as a shell starts a job.

```c++
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/io.hpp"
#include "ca/process.hpp"
#include "ca/span.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	char sh[] = "sh";
	char dash_c[] = "-c";
	char script[] = "printf hello; exit 3";
	char* const argv[]{sh, dash_c, script, nullptr};

	auto ends = ca::sys::pipe(ca::fs::cloexec_t::OPEN_VALUE);
	if (!ends)
		return 1;
	auto actions = ca::sys::spawn_actions::make();
	if (!actions || !actions->add_dup2(ends->write_end(), 1))
		return 2;
	auto child = ca::sys::posix_spawnp("sh", argv, nullptr, &*actions);
	if (!child || !actions->cleanup() || !ca::sys::close(ends->write_end()))
		return 3;

	char out[16]{};
	ca::usz total = 0U;
	for (;;) {
		auto got = ca::sys::read(ends->read_end(), ca::span<char>{out}.without_prefix(total));
		if (!got)
			return 4;
		if (*got == 0U)
			break;
		total += *got;
	}
	auto status = ca::sys::waitpid(*child);
	if (!status || status->exit_code() != 3 || status->succeeded())
		return 5;
	return ca::sv::str{out, total} == ca::sv::str{"hello"} && ca::sys::close(ends->read_end()) ? 0 : 6;
}
```

A forked child calls `exit_immediately`, which runs no handler and flushes nothing: the parent's
buffered output would otherwise be written twice.

### Signals

`sys::signals` names the signal numbers without `<signal.h>` (`interrupt`, `terminate`, `kill`,
`child`, `pipe`, `user1`, ..., and for job control `stop`, `resume`, `terminal_stop`,
`background_read`, `background_write`, `window_resize`). `sys::sigaction(signal, handler)` installs a handler with
interrupted calls restarted, or a `signal_disposition` (`default_action`, `ignore`).
`signal_set` is a constexpr set of signal numbers for `sigprocmask` (`block`, `unblock`,
`replace`) and `sigwait`, which takes a blocked signal synchronously: the safe way to handle
signals without running code inside a handler.

```c++
#include "ca/process.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::sys::signal_set set;
	set.add(ca::sys::signals::user1);
	auto previous = ca::sys::sigprocmask(ca::sys::mask_change::block, set);
	if (!previous)
		return 1;
	if (!ca::sys::kill(ca::sys::getpid(), ca::sys::signals::user1))
		return 2;
	auto taken = ca::sys::sigwait(set);
	if (!taken || *taken != ca::sys::signals::user1)
		return 3;
	return ca::sys::sigprocmask(ca::sys::mask_change::replace, *previous) ? 0 : 4;
}
```

`sigwaitinfo(set)` takes the signal with a `signal_info`: its number, how it was sent
(`code()`, `signal_codes::user` or `queue`), the sender's pid and uid, and the value
`sigqueue(pid, signal, value)` carried. `sigtimedwait(set, nanoseconds)` waits at most that long
and fails with `errc::timed_out`; 0 polls. Both, and `sigqueue`, are Linux-only
(`errc::not_supported` elsewhere).

`sigaction(signal, handler, flags)` takes `sigaction_flags` (`restart`, `on_alternate_stack`,
`no_defer`, `reset_handler`) instead of the default restart. A handler meant to survive a stack
overflow runs `on_alternate_stack`, on the memory `sigaltstack(stack)` gives the thread; an empty
span removes it, which a thread must do before that memory goes away.

```c++
#include "ca/process.hpp"
#include "ca/span.hpp"

static char alternate[65536];

static auto on_overflow(int) -> void {
	ca::sys::exit_immediately(70);
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	if (!ca::sys::sigaltstack(ca::span<char>{alternate}))
		return 1;
	if (!ca::sys::sigaction(ca::sys::signals::segv, on_overflow, ca::sys::sigaction_flags::on_alternate_stack))
		return 2;
	ca::sys::signal_set set;
	set.add(ca::sys::signals::user1);
	if (!ca::sys::sigprocmask(ca::sys::mask_change::block, set))
		return 3;
	if (!ca::sys::sigqueue(ca::sys::getpid(), ca::sys::signals::user1, 7))
		return 4;
	auto info = ca::sys::sigtimedwait(set, 1000000000);
	return info && info->queued() && info->value() == 7 ? 0 : 5;
}
```

### Identity and resources

`getpid`, `getppid`, `getuid`, `geteuid`, `getgid`, `getegid`, `getpgid`, `setpgid` and
`setsid` cover process identity and groups. `getrlimit` and `setrlimit` read and set a
`resource_limit` (soft `current()` and hard `maximum()`, `UNLIMITED` for none) of a `resource`
(`open_files`, `stack_size`, `address_space`, ...). `getrusage` reports a `resource_usage` of the
process or of its waited-for children: CPU times in nanoseconds, peak resident memory, page
faults and context switches.

```c++
#include "ca/process.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto files = ca::sys::getrlimit(ca::sys::resource::open_files);
	if (!files || files->current() == 0U)
		return 1;
	if (!ca::sys::setrlimit(ca::sys::resource::open_files, *files))
		return 2;
	auto usage = ca::sys::getrusage(ca::sys::usage_target::process);
	return usage && usage->max_resident() > 0U ? 0 : 3;
}
```
